content.text:
Now let me also prevent the text selection on the app chrome (the header, the panel) — on mobile, dragging over the text selects it, which looks broken.

Let me add a global rule for the app:
```css
.app {
  -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;  /* hmm, on buttons this removes the feedback */
}
```

Let me be careful: the tap highlight on buttons is a useful affordance. But on the text elements it's noise.

Let me do:
- The app chrome (text) → user-select: none (so dragging doesn't select text)
- The buttons → keep the tap highlight but make it subtle (use the accent color with low opacity)

Let me add:
```css
/* Auf dem Smartphone markiert der Browser sonst beim Wischen Text –
   auf einer Spieloberfläche ist das nie gewollt. */
.app {
  user-select: none;
  -webkit-user-select: none;
}

/* Der Tap-Highlight der Schaltflächen wird dezent in der Akzentfarbe
   gezeigt statt als graues Rechteck. */
.btn,
.icon-btn,
.segmented button,
input {
  -webkit-tap-highlight-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 22%, transparent);
}
```

Hmm, `color-mix` is supported in modern browsers ✓ (I already use it in the CSS for the overlay background).

Also, the input fields should still be selectable (the user might want to edit the seed) → `input { user-select: text; }`.

